1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,547
Chapter two — designing a system.

2
00:00:03,607 --> 00:00:07,353
An empty canvas, and three ways to fill it.

3
00:00:07,413 --> 00:00:14,045
First: describe what you want.

4
00:00:14,105 --> 00:00:27,466
One sentence. No diagram, no template.

5
00:00:27,526 --> 00:00:46,913
That is a real call to Claude,
against the live model.

6
00:00:46,973 --> 00:01:44,858
It answers with a spec — every node
typed, wired and given its dials.

7
00:01:44,918 --> 00:01:51,362
Sized, wired and costed the moment it lands.

8
00:01:51,422 --> 00:01:59,580
Second: by hand, from the catalog on the left.

9
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:08,513
Search it, then drag it onto the canvas.

10
00:02:08,573 --> 00:02:14,515
A node arrives already sized and
priced — never as an empty shape.

11
00:02:14,575 --> 00:02:19,836
Ports on each side are the data
flow. Drag from one to the other.

12
00:02:19,896 --> 00:02:28,538
That edge is not a line on a drawing — it
carries protocol, port, bandwidth and cost.

13
00:02:28,598 --> 00:02:35,229
Third: the spec itself.

14
00:02:35,289 --> 00:02:43,736
Every design is JSON — nodes, edges,
workload — and it is editable.

15
00:02:43,796 --> 00:02:58,548
Paste one in, or run slash architect in
Claude Code and press Load generated.

16
00:02:58,608 --> 00:03:04,953
Same result, no backend and no API key needed.

17
00:03:05,013 --> 00:03:13,225
However it got here, Analyze
reads the whole thing.

18
00:03:13,285 --> 00:03:23,730
It builds a skill card for every node, adds
the live metrics, and sends the lot to Claude.

19
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:37,395
And there is the verdict — findings
against the design you actually built.

20
00:03:37,455 --> 00:03:50,559
Each one names the node it came
from, so you can go and fix it.

21
00:03:50,619 --> 00:03:59,918
Re-run it after a change,
or open what it was given.

22
00:03:59,978 --> 00:04:03,720
Describe it, draw it, or declare it.

23
00:04:03,780 --> 00:04:07,781
The model underneath is the same either way.
